/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67074685-00b3-5b87-a998-db4ac8a3d493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469834} :answer-field/id #uuid "062d6788-0eeb-5314-b696-d606d4d46c78" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "29b22caa-a284-5c7f-8948-ff4f4a184ce4" :question/math-academy-id "q-96745" :question/problem "Which of the following functions has the domain ${1,2}?$\n- **I.** $p:{1,2}\\to R$\n- **II.** $q:R\\to [1,2)$\n- **III.** $r:[1,2)\\to {1,2}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The domain of $p$ is ${1,2},$ the set containing the two numbers $1$ and $2$ only.\n\n- The domain of $q$ is $R,$ the set of real numbers.\n\n- The domain of $r$ is $[1,2),$ the interval from $1$ to $2$ (including $1$ but not including $2$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360706 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172031 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbac348b-629f-52a1-9bb3-0d3f8483ff44" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Arrow Function Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab395d8f-61f1-5f50-9566-1db2ff9700da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469836} {:answer/id #uuid "09929bdc-611f-5115-8599-845fd139ebe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 1759218604549